.SH EXIT STATUS
Normally,
one should interpret
.BR \%@TPUT@ "'s"
exit statuses as follows.
.PP
.if n .ne 3
.if t .ne 2
.TS
Lb Lb
Lb Lx.
Status	Meaning When \-S Not Specified
_
0	T{
Boolean or string capability present,
or any numeric \%capability
T}
1	Boolean or string capability absent
2	T{
usage error,
no terminal type specified,
or \fBclear\fP operand and no \fBclear\fP capability
T}
3	unrecognized terminal type
4	unrecognized capability code
>4	system error (4 + \fBerrno\fP)
.TE
.PP
A numeric capability gives status 0 whether or not the terminal has it;
an absent one is written as
.BR \-1 "."
Given more than one operand,
.B \%@TPUT@
stops at the first one whose status is not 0,
and exits with that status.
.PP
When the
.B \-S
option is used,
some statuses change meanings.
.PP
.if n .ne 4
.if t .ne 3
.TS
Lb Lb
Lb Lx.
Status	Meaning When \-S Specified
_
0	all operands interpreted
1	unused
4	T{
unrecognized capability code;
later operands not interpreted
T}
>4	T{
4 + the number of operands whose status was not 0,
or system error (4 + \fBerrno\fP)
T}
.TE
